WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.710
Hi! I'm Erik Voss, and the new movie, Baby
Driver, is one of the most thrilling,

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.370
entertaining films I've ever seen.
Director Edgar Wright, who also made Shaun

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.580
of the Dead and Scott Pilgrim vs. the
World, is one of my favorite filmmakers.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.560
His whole visual style, and the way he
has been increasingly setting his films

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.420
to music, with choreographed action scenes
that play out like music videos.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.199
Now with Baby Driver, Wright takes this method to
a whole new level, writing the full 35

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:32.820
song soundtrack into the script, and
letting each song dictate the narrative

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.390
of the scene that it's in. Now, this is the
opposite of how music normally works in

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.059
films. Traditionally, scenes are written
and shot and edited, and then a composer

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:45.329
composes the score to the scene. He said
he was actually warned by director

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:49.170
Quentin Tarantino not to write the exact
songs into the script before shooting,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.070
because often it's hard for the studio
to secure the rights to use these songs.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.449
But Wright did it anyway. And he blasted
this music over speakers on set, and had

00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:01.350
actors and crew people listen to
synchronized ipods. And the end result

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.339
is that it all plays out like an action
musical. Like Stomp, but with cars.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:09.659
Honestly, it feels a lot more like after
you see a Fast and Furious movie, and

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:13.470
you're driving home and some great song
comes over the radio, and then suddenly

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:19.439
you're in a movie, ba-da-da-da-da-da-da-da,
then you're getting pulled over, and you start

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:24.479
crying because cops intimidate you... So,
let's look at the most interesting

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:29.040
examples of how Edgar Wright uses
specific music to construct a scene in

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.060
Baby Driver. And spoiler warning, if you
haven't seen the movie yet. Seriously, go

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.509
watch it. Okay, let's start with the
opening images of the film. The music in

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:42.439
this opening heist scene is Bell-Bottoms,
by John Spencer Blues Explosion.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.920
Edgar Wright actually said this song is what
inspired the whole film. Back in the 90s

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.660
when he was 21 and broke, he listened to
Bell-Bottoms and noticed how it starts

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:00.340
out with a steady blues riff and then
two minutes in, it explodes into this

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:09.670
frantic garage rock jam. He said how this
shift in the music made him visualize an

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.490
entire car chase film scene, and then
years later in 2011, after Wright made a

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.510
few movies that were successful, he
directed a music video for Mint Royale

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:28.330
called Blue Song. And he said that that
vision in his head transformed into this

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.709
concept of a getaway driver waiting
while a heist goes down, rocking out alone

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:36.880
in a car to the beat of the music, with
the song used as a timer for the heist.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.050
He actually planned for this music video
to be the opening scene of his eventual

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.680
film, Baby Driver, and six years later,
that's pretty much what he did. The first

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.850
two minutes of Bell Bottoms is the timer
for Buddy, Darling and Griff to rob

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.750
the bank. And then when they return, the
music kick arrives and the chase begins,

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.890
with each tire screech and police siren
all set to the beat of the music. Now

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.459
this synchronization might have reminded
you of the trailers for Suicide Squad,

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:06.060
the way gunfire and punches all
correspond to Bohemian Rhapsody,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.739
However, Wright actually said that this is
even more of a challenge than Suicide

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:15.519
Squad trailers were, because instead of
an editor just matching up shots to

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:20.140
music in an editing bay after the fact,
he did this process on the set, in the

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.340
direction of the actors and the stunt
drivers. And yeah, this is way harder, but

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.480
Wright did say it also saved money,
because storyboarding each sequence into

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.260
sections of a song forced them to think
more mathematically, because then there's

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.830
zero-fat when you're shooting it. Like,
the song won't get any longer, so the

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:39.489
stunts have to be exact and stay within
budget. Okay, now let's move on to the

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:43.510
sequence that comes immediately after: a
three-minute tracking shot following

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.410
Baby on his morning walk to get coffee,
set to the music of Harlem Shuffle by

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:54.330
Bob and Earl.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.860
Now you may have noticed that this song
starts the same way as House of Pain's

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.340
Jump Around.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.600
And that's because House of Pain actually sampled
this exact riff from Harlem Shuffle. And

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.830
Wright said that he intentionally wanted
to catch us off-guard like this, and he

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.670
does this throughout the film, using more
obscure covers of songs, while in other

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.660
cases switching back to the lesser-known
original version. Wright also got British

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:27.030
DJ Mark Nicholson, also known as Osymyso, to compose a new mix of Harlem

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:31.650
Shuffle to blend in audio from
people on the street, footsteps, people

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.300
talking, dogs barking, an ATM beeping as
woman punches in her pin, even the actor

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.320
Ansel Elgort saying yeah yeah yeah to the beat
of the music. I really have to watch this

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.980
movie again, because even the words and
the signs and the graffiti on the walls

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.370
sync up to words that you hear in the
song at that moment. The point of all

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.900
this is to put us firmly inside Baby's
head. A car accident that he suffered as a

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.070
kid left him with a constant ringing sound,
so he's constantly listening to music to

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.640
drown it out. So when we go inside his
head, we see that the world around him is

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.900
alive with music. It's as if he's
composing all of these sounds into his

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.490
own personal life soundtrack, which is
something he literally does later in

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:12.240
this movie. Now, of course, this scene was
carefully choreographed ahead of time.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.050
Wright brought in the choreographer Ryan
Heffington, and he said he walked through

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.010
the sequence with an iPhone camera over
and over again to get it precise. The end

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:24.060
result is this amazing sequence that
looks like it could be from a musical

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.810
like La La Land. Okay, let's move on to the
next big action sequence, the second

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:32.230
heist in the truck. And the music here is
Neat Neat Neat by the Damned.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.020
Now Edgar Wright sent the script to the
actors along with the thumb drive

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:43.069
containing the soundtrack, and Flea from
the Red Hot Chili Peppers, who plays

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:47.240
Eddie No Nose in this scene, saw this
song, Neat Neat Neat, in the track list, and

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.449
that is what sealed the deal for him to
be in the film. And the fun thing about

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:54.050
this song is that it's too short. It's only
about 2 minutes long. So when they were

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:57.050
storyboarding the scene, the
cinematographer of the movie, Bill Pope,

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:00.349
told Wright that they were going to run
out of song. These trucks just couldn't

00:06:00.349 --> 00:06:04.610
move as fast as Wright would need them
to to fit within the song. So Wright said

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.719
he came up with a creative solution: he
added this shot where they have to

00:06:08.719 --> 00:06:14.330
change cars, and Baby refuses to hit the
gas until he resets the song. "Wait wait wait, I gotta

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:19.819
start the song over. Ok, go." And this is
great, because it also tells us more

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:24.110
about Baby as a character. He is that
obsessed with having music playing all

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.369
the time, even if it slows him down for a
second. And again, this is discipline by

00:06:28.369 --> 00:06:32.899
Edgar Wright. He insisted so hard on having
this music that he crafted the story and

00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:36.409
the character around it. And it led to
this great character-revealing moment.

00:06:36.409 --> 00:06:40.309
Now, we could probably look at the music
in every scene in Baby Driver like this,

00:06:40.309 --> 00:06:43.969
like even Baby meeting Debora in the
diner is structured around Carla

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:49.279
Thomas's Baby and T Rex's Debora and
Beck's Debra. And sure, I would love to dig

00:06:49.279 --> 00:06:52.069
into those, but let's stick with the
action and fast-forward to this

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:56.059
warehouse shootout. Now the music here is
Tequila but instead of the popular

00:06:56.059 --> 00:07:00.319
Champs version that everyone knows, Wright
again used a cover version, this one by

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:09.110
the Button-Down Brass. Now the middle of
this song features a dueling drum solo,

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.649
and when Wright first heard that, he said
that it would be amazing to do a

00:07:12.649 --> 00:07:16.879
gunfight scene to that. And because there
was so much gunfire in the scene, it

00:07:16.879 --> 00:07:20.479
drowned out all the music playing from
the speakers on set or in the earbuds, so

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:24.769
the choreographer and stunt guy had to
teach each actor the rhythm of the shots.

00:07:24.769 --> 00:07:27.889
And then as they repeatedly shot the
scene, they had to keep counting them in,

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:32.599
like 5 6 7 8 bang bang ba-bang. And Wright
said that on one take when he was

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:36.259
checking in with Jamie Foxx to make sure
he had the gunfire rhythm down, Foxx was

00:07:36.259 --> 00:07:40.849
walking him through it, saying this guy
goes up ba-ba-ba, and I go ba-ba, and then

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:44.659
he jokingly mimed taking a drag on a
cigarette, and Wright loved that invisible

00:07:44.659 --> 00:07:48.320
cigarette so much that he had Foxx do it
for the movie. So yeah, this is

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.620
another example of this meticulous
choreography to the music

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.700
leading to a really fun, memorable image
that Wright probably wouldn't've had in the

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.480
film otherwise. Okay, let's take a look at
one last great example of Wright's use

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.440
of music in Baby Driver later in the
movie - the foot chase. The music here is

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.140
Focus by Hocus Pocus. God, I love saying that.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.180
Now Wright said that this was his
favorite part of the movie, but that the

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.780
studio actually wanted to cut it; they
refused to pay for the scene, but he

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:24.169
wanted the scene so bad that he paid out
of pocket for two extra days of shooting

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:28.100
to film it. Now, Wright said that he first
heard Hocus Pocus on a music show in

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.060
England, and that he was blown away by
this progressive rock song that, unlike

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:36.289
most songs, changes tempo throughout the
song. Now, like most songs just have one

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:39.860
tempo that plays all the way through,
which isn't really helpful for film

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.640
scoring, since the action changes so fast.
But Wright described Focus as the

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:48.170
perfect cardio track that dictates where
Baby goes. Like fast guitars for when

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:51.350
he's sprinting, and then this yodeling
section for when he's hiding behind a

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:54.529
tree, and then another shift when he's
running through the mall, and then an

00:08:54.529 --> 00:08:58.880
accordion section when he breaks into a
car. Now these were just the big examples

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.870
of creative music editing that stood out
to me, but what was your favorite musical

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:06.830
moment in Baby Driver? Also, are there any
other movies or TV shows that you think

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.730
would have benefited from this approach
of letting music dictate the story?

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:15.589
Really, there is so much going on in Baby
Driver, and I would love to do a full

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:18.529
breakdown of all the missable stuff
that I caught, but really, just more

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:21.709
people need to go see this movie, and you
